hello and congratulations jay  hi barry nice to meet you thank you very  much so i have a feeling this is  probably going to be the thing that most  are wondering about after seeing the  movie  does this really happen to people how  much truth is there  in the operation that marla's running uh  in mother's operation a lot of it  happens unfortunately it's uh  it's true true to life and the fact that  there are lots of  these predatory guardians who do prey on  vulnerable and elderly people and sort  of  entrap them in these guardianships and  uh basically sort of strip their life  apart  it's uh the true life stories of it uh  really quite harrowing and horrifying  um so unfortunately yeah it does it does  happen  is there any particular true detail you  learned about that kind of scenario  while you were prepping for this that  maybe you didn't have the screen time to  include in the movie that you wanted to  not really i mean the sort of the aim of  the movie wasn't really to do an expose  on this  on this uh scam it was more to you know  show this scam and show how it's sort of  like an example of what  what kind of happens in a in a in a  world which is  sort of focused on like um on success  and on efficiency you know and on  trusting people that you already know  you know so like the judge  works with marla all the time so like  likes her and understands her and  trusts her in a way that he doesn't know  the family member and doesn't trust them  and family members emotional  whereas marla's not she's efficient and  and very well put together i mean  one thing i we spoke about um while  we're making the film i spoke to  rosamund about  which maybe you know you sort of get a  sense of it but it didn't really make  the final film which is  if you met mother in real life and like  i say at a party or something you'd like  just think well she's like  very attractive or even put together  well-dressed you know she seems to have  a great relationship caring relationship  with her  girlfriend and she you know she's got  great taste i mean her office is  beautiful and she's kind of smart and  you know she seems she seems really um  really great person and then you'd ask  her what do you do for a living and she  goes well you know i take care of  elderly people and you think this  this lady is great she's a saint but you  know part of that you know that thing of  like  people looking to the world as if  they're like good people who do  good things but actually there's lots of  bad things underneath the hood i think  is something that is very true of that  world and true of lots of different  people in  positions of authority who get away with  a lot because people just trust them  because they're  oh they're the guy that we know you know  so there's that element that we didn't  quite make into the film but  i think is very true in that case then  on the flip side of it  what drew you to having you know this  protagonist that's doing a bad  thing having her go up against someone  or at least  target someone who is also doing a bad  thing  rather than having made jennifer a you  know a  more traditional innocent party  i mean i think jennifer's innocent of  whatever  you know marla's doing to her she  doesn't deserve that i don't think  anybody deserves that i don't think uh  you know whatever you done in your life  you don't deserve bad things to happen  to you  kind of by a con person but i think um  marla's character and these characters  in real life sort of struck me as being  a little bit like gangsters you know  they come in and they they steal  something  under false pretenses and then they sort  of strip it for parts and then  you know pump all the money out of it as  they can you know when they've got  assets they  they use those assets and overcharge and  overcharge another charge and when they  run out of those assets and assets and  they get put onto  you know sort of government money they  then just store them in like the worst  care home they sort of run through their  money fast as possible and store them in  the worst care home and just forget  about them just park them and then move  on to the next one  that felt almost like you know a  gangster's operation  and so the idea of taking somebody who's  basically um  a legal gangster because there's not  really anything she's doing that's  totally against the law she's  overcharging people but she's sort of  exposing loopholes in the law running up  against somebody who  is a gangster and like is on the other  side of the law felt  quite interesting and like because he  has like violence and ruthlessness  and like criminality on his side where  she is on the right side of the law and  how different are they so  having this sort of yin and yang of like  of immorality  pitted against each other and seeing put  them in sort of like a death match of  who's going to give up first  seemed uh like a bit of a delicious  prospect  that it was uh before i get kicked out  of here i did want to ask you more of a  broader career question because  i saw alice creed at tribeca and i've  been following your work ever since  and i know that jumping from alice creed  to the fifth wave was a big step up as  far as you know scope and scale goes but  of course that also comes with  higher risk and expectations and maybe  studio involvement so  how did the reception to that one wind  up  influencing the next project you wanted  to do i mean i think it was it was less  the reception the more of my experience  of it  i mean on alice creed i'd written it and  so i was totally across the script and  was sort of  as the writer and director of it i uh i  sort of had the  the parent authority because it was on a  lower budget to make the film i wanted  to make and just sort of necessarily as  like a smaller filmmaker going to a big  studio  uh i didn't have the the authority or  the power that i had on the smaller film  and you know that's that would happen to  any filmmaker in any studio i think of a  similar sort of jump up and some sort of  you know um similar material that's  going to be released in every cinema  in the world you know so i think what  that did is sort of  solidify in my head that the thing that  i really like to do  is to write the scripts and have can  have like authority over the script and  then move forward and direct it that way  it feels like the same the same job to  me whereas i didn't have that same job  on the fifth wave so  although i learned a huge amount from  making the fifth wave um  as you will do is like going to like a  big budget  film school but getting paid for it you  know what i mean and some brilliant  actors i work in some brilliant  people on the crew who i worked with i  think that i found it much more  satisfying to  to have written and directed so coming  back and like you know  10 years after i agreed to make i care a  lot it felt much more of  you know familiar of like you know the  material being  more me and i think that was more  rewarding in the end ten years after i  still quite like when you write and  direct  a movie so huge congratulations on this  i absolutely loved it  thank you barry thank you

